# Target C-II — exclude all higher-codimension ground-collinearity strata in reciprocal `H5`

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the noncollapsed degree-pure support-six reciprocal `H5` endpoint after the uniform/simple-wall finite reduction  
**Depends on:** `target_c_support6_reciprocal_h5_reduction_2026-09-18.md`; bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem; local hull `2+3` sign alternation  
**Regression:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_reciprocal_h5_double_wall_exclusion_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding `H5` reduction left higher-codimension configurations with more than one ground-ray coincidence untreated. This note removes them completely.

An allowed ground collinearity `0,i,j` forces `ij` to be a missing matching edge. Since no three non-ground reciprocal points are collinear, ground-ray coincidence classes have size at most two. In an `H5` reciprocal hull a coincident pair cannot consist of two hull vertices, so each pair consumes an interior reciprocal point. There are only two interior points. Hence at most two such pairs can occur.

If two occur, symmetry forces the unique configuration

\[
\boxed{25\text{ tied at the ground},\qquad 36\text{ tied at the ground},\qquad M=\{14,25,36\}.}
\]

The ground equilibrium then fixes the signs, up to global reversal, to

\[
\boxed{s_{01}=-,
\quad s_{02}=s_{05}=+,
\quad s_{03}=s_{06}=-,
\quad s_{04}=+.}
\]

Convexity fixes enough of the local radial order at hull vertices 2 and 3 that the existing positions-two-and-four alternation theorem gives contradictory signs for the present reciprocal edge `23`: vertex 2 forces `s_{23}=-`, while vertex 3 forces `s_{23}=+`.

Therefore no double ground-collinearity stratum exists. Since triple or higher coincidence is already impossible, the `H5` nonuniform analysis is complete:

\[
\boxed{\text{every noncollapsed support-six reciprocal `H5` block is either uniform or lies on one simple wall.}}
\]

Combined with the preceding finite reduction, the entire noncollapsed `H5` branch is now reduced to exactly six uniform and ten simple-wall sign/order symmetry orbits. The simple `056` wall is empty.

---

## 1. Allowed ground coincidences form disjoint missing-matching pairs

The support-six reciprocal theorem already proves that no three non-ground reciprocal points are collinear.

If `0,i,j` are collinear and `ij` is a present relation edge, then at physical pentagon `F_i` the outside anchor `i0` and relation train `ij` are projectively parallel. The bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem forbids this. Hence every ground-collinear pair satisfies

\[
\boxed{ij\in M.}
\tag{1.1}
\]

Since `M` is a perfect matching, distinct ground coincidences are disjoint.

Because all non-ground points lie in the ground hull cone of width `<pi/3`, a ground-collinear pair lies on one common ray from `0`, not opposite rays. Two distinct hull vertices cannot lie on one such ray: the nearer point would lie on the segment from `0` to the farther point and would not be an extreme point of the reciprocal hull. Thus every coincident pair contains at least one of the two interior reciprocal points `5,6`.

It follows that there can be at most two ground-collinear pairs.

If there are two, both interior points are consumed, one per pair. An interior reciprocal point cannot lie on either boundary ray `01` or `04`, because it would lie on the boundary of the reciprocal hull. Therefore each interior point pairs with hull vertex `2` or `3`. After interchanging `5,6` if necessary, the only possibility is

\[
\boxed{25,\ 36.}
\tag{1.2}
\]

The remaining matching edge is forced to be `14`, so

\[
\boxed{M=\{14,25,36\}.}
\tag{1.3}
\]

Thus (1.2)--(1.3) are the unique higher-codimension `H5` stratum up to the existing symmetries.

---

## 2. Ground equilibrium on the double wall

Write the four distinct ground unit rays in increasing angular order as

\[
u_1,\quad u_A,\quad u_B,\quad u_4,
\]

where

\[
u_A=u_2=u_5,
\qquad
u_B=u_3=u_6.
\]

All four angles lie in an open interval of width `<pi/3`.

The ground sign split is still exactly `3+3`: the earlier cone projection argument ruling out `0+6`, `1+5`, and `2+4` uses only cone width and unit magnitudes, not distinctness of the rays.

Neither duplicate pair can have opposite signs. If both pairs had opposite signs, they cancel and the two remaining singleton rays would have to cancel, impossible because `u_1` and `u_4` are distinct and non-antipodal.

If exactly one duplicate pair had opposite signs, the other four signs consist of a same-sign duplicate pair and two singleton signs of the opposite color. Ground equilibrium would then have the form

\[
2u_A=u_1+u_4
\]

or its sign-swapped analogue. But `u_1\ne u_4`, so `|u_1+u_4|<2`, whereas `|2u_A|=2`. Contradiction.

Hence each duplicate pair has one common sign. Since the total count is `3+3`, the two duplicate pairs have opposite signs, and the two singleton rays also have opposite signs.

Globally reverse all signs if necessary and suppose the `A` pair is positive and the `B` pair negative. If `u_1` is positive and `u_4` negative, equilibrium gives

\[
2(u_B-u_A)=-(u_4-u_1).
\tag{2.1}
\]

Both `u_B-u_A` and `u_4-u_1` are forward chord vectors of the unit circle whose endpoint angles lie in one interval of width `<pi`. Their arguments lie in one open interval of width `<pi`; they cannot point in opposite directions. Equation (2.1) is impossible.

Consequently the only ground signs, up to global reversal, are

\[
\boxed{
u_1:-,
\quad u_2=u_5:+,
\quad u_3=u_6:-,
\quad u_4:+.
}
\tag{2.2}
\]

---

## 3. Hull vertex 2 forces `s_23=-`

At hull vertex `2`, the missing matching mate is `5`. Since `5` lies on segment `02`, its raw ray from `2` coincides with ray `20` and is deleted before the local five-ray rule is applied.

For the convex hull `0,1,2,3,4`, the hull rays from vertex `2` occur from one boundary of the hull cone to the other in the order

\[
3,\ 4,\ 0,\ 1.
\]

Point `6` lies on segment `03`, so its ray from `2` lies strictly between rays `23` and `20`. Its position relative to ray `24` can vary, but after deleting `5` the five present raw rays have one of the two orders

\[
3,4,6,0,1
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
3,6,4,0,1.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Thus edge `20` is in raw position four and edge `23` is in raw position one.

By (2.2), `s_{20}=+`. The local hull alternation theorem says the size-two sign class occupies positions two and four. Since position four is positive, the positive class is the size-two class. Position one is therefore negative:

\[
\boxed{s_{23}=-.}
\tag{3.2}
\]

---

## 4. Hull vertex 3 forces `s_23=+`

At hull vertex `3`, the missing matching mate is `6`. Since `6` lies on segment `03`, deleting it removes the ray tied with `30`.

The hull rays from vertex `3` occur in order

\[
4,\ 0,\ 1,\ 2.
\]

Point `5` lies on segment `02`, so its ray from `3` lies strictly between rays `30` and `32`. Its position relative to ray `31` can vary. Hence after deleting `6`, the five present rays have one of the two orders

\[
4,0,5,1,2
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
4,0,1,5,2.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Thus edge `30` is in raw position two and edge `32` is in raw position five.

By (2.2), `s_{30}=-`. Position two belongs to the size-two sign class, so here the negative class is the size-two class. Position five belongs to the opposite size-three class and is therefore positive:

\[
\boxed{s_{32}=+.}
\tag{4.2}
\]

But `s_{32}=s_{23}` because reciprocal edge signs are undirected. Equations (3.2) and (4.2) contradict each other. The global sign-reversed case gives the same contradiction with both predictions reversed.

Therefore the double ground-collinearity stratum is empty.

---

## 5. Consequence

Section 1 shows that more than two ground-collinear pairs are impossible in `H5`; Sections 2--4 exclude the unique two-pair case. Hence every noncollapsed `H5` reciprocal block is either uniform or has exactly one allowed ground-ray coincidence.

Combining this theorem with `target_c_support6_reciprocal_h5_reduction_2026-09-18.md` gives the complete sign/order reduction

\[
\boxed{\text{noncollapsed support-six `H5`}\Longrightarrow 6\text{ uniform orbits }\cup 10\text{ simple-wall orbits}.}
\]

There are no omitted higher-codimension ground-collinearity strata.

This still does not prove the sixteen surviving orbits metrically realizable or impossible. The remaining noncollapsed support-six task is exactly to solve those sixteen finite metric unit-force cases. The collapsed branch `y_1=\cdots=y_6\ne0` remains separate.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** all higher-codimension ground-collinearity strata in the reciprocal `H5` branch are excluded. C-II remains open.
